	<ai_information>
		<status verified="true" trusted="true" tampered="false" uses="true" analyzed="true" log_truncated="false" timestamp="1780672116" algorithm="ed25519" keyurl="https://iseesystems.com/keys/stella01.txt" want_var_info="true" want_var_status="true" want_log="true" sampling="1" signature="USLPna+hT/3TsKLJA/2PvWMcN5e6siThitgAO5G5B2IC9BLCiZTmFoeI7yegJoAOJY8AMHmtOOTqRpC5KtGtDg=="/>
		<log>&lt;h3&gt;You asked me...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;My reply...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;To make your Causal Loop Diagram (CLD) highly readable and professional, we can categorize its components by sector (the thematic modules of your Integrated Assessment Model) and loop polarity. Here is a recommended color legend for your CLD:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h4&gt;1. Node Colors (By Model Sector)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #2E7D32;&quot;&gt;Green (Economic Sector):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/b&gt; Represents economic activity, investments, and policy decisions. This includes: &lt;i&gt;Gross Economic Output&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Net Economic Output&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Capital Investment&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;savings rate&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Emissions Control Rate&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Abatement Cost&lt;/i&gt;, and &lt;i&gt;Economic Damages Fraction&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #1565C0;&quot;&gt;Blue (Climate &amp; Carbon Cycle Sector):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/b&gt; Represents physical climate states, forcing, and natural carbon pools. This includes: &lt;i&gt;Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Radiative Forcing&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Global Mean Temperature&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;Permafrost Thaw Rate&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;sequestration capacity&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;carbon fertilization&lt;/i&gt;, and &lt;i&gt;Industrial CO2 Emissions&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;h4&gt;2. Link Colors (By Relationship Polarity)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #1976D2;&quot;&gt;Blue Arrows (+):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/b&gt; Positive causal relationships, where the effect moves in the same direction as the cause (e.g., an increase in Capital Investment increases Gross Economic Output).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #D32F2F;&quot;&gt;Red Arrows (-):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/b&gt; Negative causal relationships, where the effect moves in the opposite direction of the cause (e.g., an increase in the Emissions Control Rate decreases Industrial CO2 Emissions).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;h4&gt;3. Loop Identifier Colors (By Feedback Type)&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #E65100;&quot;&gt;Orange/Red Identifiers (Reinforcing Loops):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/b&gt; Loops &lt;b&gt;R1&lt;/b&gt; (Permafrost Feedback) and &lt;b&gt;R2&lt;/b&gt; (Sink Saturation Feedback) represent self-amplifying processes that accelerate warming.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #00838F;&quot;&gt;Teal/Blue Identifiers (Balancing Loops):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/b&gt; Loops &lt;b&gt;B1&lt;/b&gt; (Carbon Fertilization) and &lt;b&gt;B2&lt;/b&gt; (Climate Damage GDP Limit) represent self-correcting or stabilizing processes.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;********************&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;You asked me...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;My reply...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;Adding a legend to your model&apos;s charts and diagrams is a great way to improve clarity. Since your question is about visual formatting and presentation rather than the structural dynamics of your feedback loops, I have set the feedback requirement to false for this specific request.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;To add a legend to your charts, follow these steps:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;Double-click on the chart or output canvas where you want the legend to appear to open its properties panel.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;In the chart settings menu, look for the &apos;Legend&apos; or &apos;Display Options&apos; tab.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Check the box that says &apos;Show Legend&apos; or &apos;Enable Legend&apos;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Choose your preferred placement for the legend, such as &apos;Bottom&apos;, &apos;Right&apos;, or &apos;Top&apos; of the chart area.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Make sure each of your key variables, such as &lt;b&gt;Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;Global Mean Temperature&lt;/b&gt;, and &lt;b&gt;Net Economic Output&lt;/b&gt;, has a clear display name so they are easily identifiable in the legend.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;********************&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;You instructed me to...&lt;/h3&gt;can you add a legend?
&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Here is what I have done...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Closed Loop DICE Lenton SCM Integration&lt;/h3&gt;We expanded the economic-climate model by explicitly closing multiple feedback loops. First, the classic climate damage loop is fully closed: higher Net Economic Output drives Capital Investment, increasing Gross Economic Output and Industrial CO2 Emissions, which raises Atmospheric CO2 Concentration, Radiative Forcing, Global Mean Temperature, and Economic Damages, ultimately suppressing Net Economic Output. Second, we fully closed the permafrost feedback loop where warming accelerates the Permafrost Thaw Rate, releasing more carbon and raising Atmospheric CO2 Concentration. Finally, we captured the biophysical feedback loop involving carbon fertilization and temperature-dependent sequestration capacity.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;********************&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;You asked me...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;My reply...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;p&gt;To incorporate decreased carbon sequestration capacity and the carbon fertilization effect into our System Dynamics model, we must expand our carbon-cycle representation beyond the simple atmospheric concentration. We can achieve this by introducing a land/biosphere carbon stock that interacts dynamically with atmospheric CO2.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Step-by-Step Implementation Guide:&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Introduce Carbon Fertilization:&lt;/b&gt; Create an auxiliary variable, &lt;i&gt;Net Primary Productivity (NPP)&lt;/i&gt;, which represents the uptake of carbon by the terrestrial biosphere. Express NPP as a function of &lt;b&gt;Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/b&gt; to capture the carbon fertilization effect (where higher CO2 levels stimulate plant growth and carbon absorption, creating a stabilizing negative feedback loop).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Introduce Decreased Sequestration Capacity:&lt;/b&gt; To capture the degradation of this sink due to climate change, make NPP or the land biosphere absorption rate decrease as a function of &lt;b&gt;Global Mean Temperature&lt;/b&gt;. This represents how extreme heat, changing precipitation patterns, and forest dieback decrease the land&apos;s capacity to hold carbon.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Update the Mass Balance:&lt;/b&gt; Direct a portion of atmospheric carbon into this terrestrial sink based on these competing effects (fertilization vs. thermal degradation), modifying the net accumulation in &lt;b&gt;Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/b&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;********************&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;You asked me...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;My reply...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h4&gt;Proposed Model Enhancements: Carbon Fertilization and Decreased Sequestration Capacity&lt;/h4&gt;&lt;p&gt;To capture the dual effects of &lt;b&gt;Carbon Fertilization&lt;/b&gt; (a negative feedback loop) and &lt;b&gt;Decreased Carbon Sequestration Capacity&lt;/b&gt; (a positive feedback loop), we must introduce new structure to represent the terrestrial and oceanic carbon sinks and how they respond to temperature and CO2 concentration.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Step-by-Step Implementation Guide&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Define Carbon Fertilization:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Create an auxiliary variable &lt;i&gt;Carbon Fertilization Effect&lt;/i&gt; that increases as &lt;i&gt;Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/i&gt; rises. This increases the &lt;i&gt;Net Primary Productivity (NPP)&lt;/i&gt; or &lt;i&gt;Carbon Sequestration Rate&lt;/i&gt;, which acts as a buffer (negative feedback, reducing the accumulation of CO2 in the atmosphere).&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Define Decreased Sequestration Capacity:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;Create a variable &lt;i&gt;Sequestration Capacity Fraction&lt;/i&gt; or &lt;i&gt;Temperature Sequestration Penalty&lt;/i&gt;. As &lt;i&gt;Global Mean Temperature&lt;/i&gt; rises, the capacity of terrestrial vegetation and oceans to absorb CO2 decreases (due to soil respiration outstripping NPP, ocean stratification, and solubility changes). This reduces the &lt;i&gt;Carbon Sequestration Rate&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Formulate the Net Carbon Sequestration Rate:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;The &lt;i&gt;Net Carbon Sequestration Rate&lt;/i&gt; can be modeled as:&lt;br&gt;&lt;code&gt;Net Carbon Sequestration Rate = Base Sequestration Rate * Carbon Fertilization Effect * (1 - Temperature Sequestration Penalty)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;br&gt;This rate is then subtracted from the net emissions entering the &lt;i&gt;Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/i&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Expected Dynamic Effects&lt;/h5&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Carbon Fertilization (New Negative Loop):&lt;/b&gt; Stabilizes atmospheric CO2 in the short term as concentration increases.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;b&gt;Sinking Sinks (New Positive Loop):&lt;/b&gt; As temperature rises, the sequestration capacity decreases, reinforcing warming by leaving more CO2 in the atmosphere.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;********************&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;You instructed me to...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h3&gt;Here is what I have done...&lt;/h3&gt;&lt;h3&gt;DICE Lenton Hector Coupled Dynamics&lt;/h3&gt;Based on the provided economic-climate framework text, we couple the DICE economic engine, Lenton carbon cycle, and Hector permafrost modules into a cohesive system dynamics structure. We map the flow from gross economic output to emissions, which elevates atmospheric carbon concentrations. High atmospheric carbon increases radiative forcing, raising global temperatures. Elevated temperatures drive economic damages (which reduce net economic output and subsequent investment in capital, creating a negative feedback on growth) and trigger climate-carbon feedbacks. Specifically, higher temperatures accelerate permafrost thaw (which increases soil respiration and atmospheric CO2) and stimulate other biogeochemical carbon feedbacks. Economic abatement policy (mitigation rate) acts to reduce emissions at the cost of immediate economic output, establishing the fundamental balancing loops of climate action and reinforcing loops of economic growth and ecological feedback.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;1. Gross Economic Output --&gt;(+) Industrial CO2 Emissions&lt;/h4&gt;Industrial emissions are calculated as the product of gross economic output, the emission control rate, and carbon intensity.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;Higher gross economic output directly increases industrial emissions, assuming carbon intensity and abatement rates are positive.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;2. Emissions Control Rate --&gt;(-) Industrial CO2 Emissions&lt;/h4&gt;The emissions control rate represents the fraction of emissions abated, directly mitigating gross emissions.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increase in the emissions control rate reduces the total amount of industrial CO2 emissions released into the atmosphere.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;3. Emissions Control Rate --&gt;(+) Abatement Cost&lt;/h4&gt;The abatement cost function relates the fraction of emissions abated to a corresponding reduction in economic output.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increase in the emissions control rate requires higher investment in clean technologies, increasing total abatement costs.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;4. Industrial CO2 Emissions --&gt;(+) Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/h4&gt;Industrial emissions act as a physical flow entering the atmospheric carbon pool.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;Higher emissions directly increase the concentration of CO2 in the atmosphere.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;5. Atmospheric CO2 Concentration --&gt;(+) Radiative Forcing&lt;/h4&gt;Atmospheric CO2 concentration drives radiative forcing as represented by the logarithmic forcing equation.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increase in atmospheric CO2 concentration increases global radiative forcing.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;6. Radiative Forcing --&gt;(+) Global Mean Temperature&lt;/h4&gt;Radiative forcing drives the temperature module, altering global temperature anomalies.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;Increased radiative forcing leads to a higher global mean temperature.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;7. Global Mean Temperature --&gt;(+) Economic Damages Fraction&lt;/h4&gt;The economic damage function maps global mean temperature increases to a fractional reduction in economic output.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;Higher temperatures result in a larger fractional damage to the economy.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;8. Economic Damages Fraction --&gt;(-) Net Economic Output&lt;/h4&gt;Net output is calculated by multiplying gross output by the undamaged fraction and subtracting abatement costs.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;A higher fraction of economic damages directly reduces net economic output.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;9. Abatement Cost --&gt;(-) Net Economic Output&lt;/h4&gt;Net economic output is reduced by the costs of carbon abatement.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;Higher abatement costs directly subtract from net economic output.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;10. Net Economic Output --&gt;(+) Capital Investment&lt;/h4&gt;A portion of net economic output is saved and reinvested back into the physical capital stock.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increase in net economic output leads to more capital investment, assuming a stable savings rate.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;11. Capital Investment --&gt;(+) Gross Economic Output&lt;/h4&gt;Accumulated physical capital is an input to the production function that determines gross economic output.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increase in capital increases gross economic output.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;12. Global Mean Temperature --&gt;(+) Permafrost Thaw Rate&lt;/h4&gt;Under the permafrost submodel, temperature increases speed up the thawing of frozen permafrost, which accelerates significantly past 2 degrees.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increase in temperature leads to an increase in the rate at which permafrost melts.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;13. Permafrost Thaw Rate --&gt;(+) Thawed Soil Carbon&lt;/h4&gt;As frozen permafrost melts, carbon shifts from the frozen permafrost pool into the thawed soil pool.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increased thaw rate directly increases the amount of carbon in the thawed soil pool.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;14. Thawed Soil Carbon --&gt;(+) Atmospheric CO2 Concentration&lt;/h4&gt;Carbon moves from the thawed soil to the atmosphere via soil respiration.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;An increase in thawed soil carbon increases the total volume of carbon released to the atmosphere.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h4&gt;15. Global Mean Temperature --&gt;(+) Thawed Soil Carbon&lt;/h4&gt;Carbon moves from thawed soil to the atmosphere proportionally to temperature due to increasing respiration rates.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;h5&gt;Polarity Reasoning&lt;/h5&gt;Higher temperatures stimulate soil respiration, accelerating the flux of carbon from the soil to the atmosphere.&lt;br/&gt;</log>
		<settings>
			<isee:setting key="qualitative/backgroundKnowledge" value="2.1.1 DICE 2023 Economic–Climate Framework
The DICE (Dynamic Integrated Climate–Economy) model is a compact integrated assessment
model formulated to simulate an efficient climate control strategy. It assumes the global popu-
lation has well-defined preferences, represented by a social welfare function, which is dynamically
optimized over multiple scenarios. It uses the basic Ramsey model of optimal economic growth with
adjustments to calculate the optimal path for savings, investment, and GHG-emission mitigation,
optimizing welfare discounted by pure rate of time preference (ρ) and elasticity of marginal utility
(η). Society invests in physical capital like machinery or infrastructure, subsequently abstaining
from consumption today in order to increase welfare in the future.
In DICE 2023, optimization occurs over climate mitigation control rate (μ) and savings rate
(s), adjusting the amount of money invested in mitigating climate change and the amount to
withhold from consumption. Then, the model derives the social cost of carbon (SCC), or economic
damages per ton of carbon dioxide equivilent (CO2E) emitted, as an analog for the return on
climate investment. The model can be interpreted either in a policy optimizing framework or as
the outcome of ideal competitive markets. Total factor productivity (TFP) growth, population,
and emissions intensity trajectories are treated as exogenous. Gross economic output (Y gross) is
calculated as
Y gross = T F P · (K/1000)1−γ · Kγ (1)
where γ is capital elasticity.
Industrial CO2 emissions in DICE 2023 are determined by the product of gross economic output,
the emission control rate (μ), and the intensity of carbon production (σ). The latter parameter
represents the carbon emissions per unit of GDP accumulated and decreases over time, reflecting the
decarbonization of the economy through technological innovation independent of explicit abatement
policy. Investment in climate change mitigation reduces the damage to the economy caused by
climate change, but impacts economic output. The cost of climate change mitigation (Cabate) is
represented by an abatement cost function that relates the fraction of emissions abated (μ) to a
corresponding reduction in economic output dependent on the cost of the backstop (non-fossil fuel)
technology (pback). The backstop cost itself declines over time at rate gback, reflecting assumed
progress in clean energy technology. pback was calibrated to results from the ENGAGE integrated
assessment study [19, 20] and is acknowledged as uncertain [3], while gback represents the authors’
judgements on the future pace of clean energy progress with no formal calibration. As cost of
abatement increases, economic output decreases.
Economic damages from climate change enter DICE 2023 through a damage function that
relates global mean temperature increase to a fractional reduction in economic output. The damage
function transforms temperature to economic damages as a fraction of economic output:
Df rac = a1 · T AT M + a2 · T AT M a3 (2)
where a3 controls the degree of nonlinearity in damages at elevated warming levels. These damages
are reflected directly in economic output (Y ):
Y = Y gross · (1 − Df rac) − Cabate (3)
3
2.1.2 Lenton Simple Climate Model (SCM)
The Lenton (2000) carbon model is a simple and mechanistic representation of the global carbon
cycle with fully transparent and tunable ecological and biogeochemical carbon-carbon and carbon-
climate feedbacks, including changes to soil and vegetation respiration, permafrost melt, ocean-
atmosphere gas exchange, and photosynthesis. It is a seven-box model of carbon pools including
atmosphere, vegetation, soil, low-latitude surface ocean, high-latitude surface ocean, intermediate
waters, and deep ocean. This model was chosen for its process-based nature and quick runtime,
which allow it to both reveal mechanistic detail about how individual feedbacks operate under a
changing climate and accommodate perturbation of multiple parameters to explore uncertainty and
scope of outcomes. Similarly to DFAIR, it is a global mean-field model, so it does not capture re-
gional heterogeneity in carbon flux processes which vary considerably across latitudes and moisture
regimes.
2.1.3 Representation of Permafrost Melt
We use Woodard et al.’s system dynamics representation of gradual permafrost thaw originally
implemented as an extension of the object-oriented simple carbon–climate emulator HECTOR
[26]. This permafrost submodel adds two extra carbon pools: frozen permafrost and thawed soil.
Carbon moves from frozen permafrost to thawed soil as it melts and carbon moves from the thawed
soil to the atmosphere proportionally to temperature due to increasing soil respiration. In addition
to this, we include the proposed abrupt permafrost thaw tipping point as quantified by Armstrong
McKay et al. such that, after crossing the temperature anomaly threshold of 2 degrees celcius, the
rate of frozen permafrost thawing increases by 40% [1].
2.1.4 Model Coupling Strategy
This coupling is fully continuous, with all model components solved jointly at each timestep, in
contrast to DICE 2023’s native 5-year discrete timestep formulation; the implications of this dis-
cretization difference for welfare estimation are discussed in Section 3. The Lenton carbon model
described above is coupled with the DICE 2023 economic model, fully replacing the DFAIR carbon
cycle. In place of the DFAIR atmospheric CO2 concentration parameter (MATD), the Lenton at-
mospheric CO2 concentration parameter (MATL) is substituted into the DFAIR radiative forcing
equation:
F = fco22x · ln(MATL/Meq)
ln(2) + Fmisc + FGHG (4)
where fco22x is the forcing from equilibrium CO2 doubling, Meq is the pre-industrial equilibrium
atmospheric carbon concentration, Fmisc is miscellaneous forcings, and FGHG is forcing from non-
CO2 greenhouse gas emissions. Both Fmisc and FGHG are exogenous variables retained from DICE
2023. All remaining DFAIR carbon cycle variables are overwritten and do not persist in the coupled
system.
Anthropogenic CO2E emissions (ECO2E), comprising both industrial emissions and land-use
change emissions from DICE 2023, are introduced as a flow into the Lenton atmospheric carbon
pool. The resulting atmospheric CO2 concentration propagates simultaneously through the Lenton
carbon cycle, modifying internal carbon fluxes, and through the radiative forcing equation above,
which drives the DFAIR temperature module. Temperature changes subsequently enter the DICE
2023 damage function, affecting estimates of the cost of climate change. This coupling is fully
simultaneous, with all model components solved jointly at each timestep.
4
2.1.5 Model Parameterization and Sensitivity
The mitigation trajectory (μ) and savings rate are held fixed at values pre-optimized under the
standard DICE 2023 framework using General Algebraic Modeling System GAMS, such that ob-
served differences in model output reflect the influence of carbon-climate feedbacks rather than
re-optimization of economic controls. Under this forcing, atmospheric CO2 concentration, global
mean temperature, and economic damages are recorded as time series, enabling direct param-
eter sweep comparisons across the three model configurations: DICE 2023 alone, DICE-L, and
DICE-LP. Atmospheric CO2 concentration, global mean temperature, economic damages, abate-
ment costs, and accumulated welfare are recorded as time series and compared directly across all
three model configurations to quantify the extent to which carbon-climate feedbacks affect physical
and economic outcomes.
These three model configurations are tested for their sensitivity to DICE damage and discount-
ing conventions. For an alternative damage function, we use the Howard-Sterner (2017) damage
coefficient (a2 = 0.01), which projects economic losses approximately three times greater than the
DICE-2023 default (a2 = 0.0035) at 3°C of warming. For the discounting sensitivity, we set the pure
rate of time preference to zero (ρ = 0), climate beta coefficient (βclim = 0) retaining consumption-
growth discounting through the elasticity of marginal utility (η = 0.95). These two alternatives
are applied factorially across all three physical model configurations, yielding twelve runs in total.
The emissions control trajectory and savings rate are held at their GAMS-optimized values in all
cases; welfare is not re-optimized under the alternative damage or discounting specifications."/>
			<isee:setting key="qualitative/problemStatement" value="Please build me a CLD for my paper joining the DICE integrated assessment with carbon-climate feedbacks from the Lenton simple climate model and the hector permafrost module"/>
			<isee:setting key="seldon/backgroundKnowledge" value=""/>
			<isee:setting key="seldon/problemStatement" value="Please add decreased carbon sequestration capacity and carbon fertilization
"/>
		</settings>
	</ai_information>
	<sim_specs isee:sim_duration="1.5" isee:run_prefix="Run" isee:simulation_delay="0.125" isee:restore_on_start="false" method="Euler" time_units="Months" isee:instantaneous_flows="false" isee:ignore_module_errors="false" isee:strict_units="true" isee:loop_scores="true" isee:loop_exhaustive_allowed="1000">
		<start>0</start>
		<stop>12</stop>
		<dt reciprocal="true">4</dt>
	</sim_specs>
	<isee:prefs saved_runs="5" keep="false" show_module_prefix="true" live_update_on_drag="true" show_restore_buttons="false" layer="cld" isee:design="Deluxe Prev-Next Blue Green Pastel" isee:design_custom="false" interface_scale_ui="true" interface_max_page_width="10000" interface_max_page_height="10000" interface_min_page_width="0" interface_min_page_height="0" rifp="true"/>
	<isee:multiplayer_settings include_chat="true" allow_observers="false" advance_time_increment="1" max_advance_time_in_ms="0" model_stops_when_players_drop="false" observer_start_page="home_page" enabled="false"/>
	<isee:time_formats default_format="Builtin">
		<isee:time_format name="Builtin" type="adaptive"/>
	</isee:time_formats>
	<isee:number_format_extenders/>
	<default_format/>
	<model_units/>
	<model>
		<variables>
			<aux name="Gross Economic Output" ai_state="C">
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units/>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Emissions Control Rate" ai_state="C">
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units/>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Industrial CO2 Emissions" ai_state="C">
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units/>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Atmospheric CO2 Concentration" ai_state="C">
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units/>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Radiative Forcing" ai_state="C">
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units/>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Global Mean Temperature" ai_state="C">
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units/>
			</aux>
			<aux name="Economic Damages Fraction" ai_state="C">
				<isee:delay_aux/>
				<units/>
