					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux x="332.191" y="503" width="161.5" height="32" name="Capital Investment">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<aux color="#919191" font_color="#919191" x="945.605" y="97.367" width="192.5" height="32" name="Permafrost Thaw Rate">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="1" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="110.317">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
Higher gross economic output directly increases industrial emissions, assuming carbon intensity and abatement rates are positive.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Industrial emissions are calculated as the product of gross economic output, the emission control rate, and carbon intensity.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>Higher gross economic output directly increases industrial emissions, assuming carbon intensity and abatement rates are positive.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Industrial emissions are calculated as the product of gross economic output, the emission control rate, and carbon intensity.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Gross_Economic_Output</from>
					<to>Industrial_CO2_Emissions</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="2" font_size="18pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="53.281">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
An increase in the emissions control rate reduces the total amount of industrial CO2 emissions released into the atmosphere.

Polarity Reasoning: 
The emissions control rate represents the fraction of emissions abated, directly mitigating gross emissions.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>An increase in the emissions control rate reduces the total amount of industrial CO2 emissions released into the atmosphere.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>The emissions control rate represents the fraction of emissions abated, directly mitigating gross emissions.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Emissions_Control_Rate</from>
					<to>Industrial_CO2_Emissions</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="3" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="281.598">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
An increase in the emissions control rate requires higher investment in clean technologies, increasing total abatement costs.

Polarity Reasoning: 
The abatement cost function relates the fraction of emissions abated to a corresponding reduction in economic output.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>An increase in the emissions control rate requires higher investment in clean technologies, increasing total abatement costs.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>The abatement cost function relates the fraction of emissions abated to a corresponding reduction in economic output.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Emissions_Control_Rate</from>
					<to>Abatement_Cost</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="4" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="15.236">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
Higher emissions directly increase the concentration of CO2 in the atmosphere.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Industrial emissions act as a physical flow entering the atmospheric carbon pool.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>Higher emissions directly increase the concentration of CO2 in the atmosphere.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Industrial emissions act as a physical flow entering the atmospheric carbon pool.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Industrial_CO2_Emissions</from>
					<to>Atmospheric_CO2_Concentration</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="5" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="358.284">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
An increase in atmospheric CO2 concentration increases global radiative forcing.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Atmospheric CO2 concentration drives radiative forcing as represented by the logarithmic forcing equation.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>An increase in atmospheric CO2 concentration increases global radiative forcing.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Atmospheric CO2 concentration drives radiative forcing as represented by the logarithmic forcing equation.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Atmospheric_CO2_Concentration</from>
					<to>Radiative_Forcing</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="6" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="256.991">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
Increased radiative forcing leads to a higher global mean temperature.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Radiative forcing drives the temperature module, altering global temperature anomalies.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>Increased radiative forcing leads to a higher global mean temperature.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Radiative forcing drives the temperature module, altering global temperature anomalies.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Radiative_Forcing</from>
					<to>Global_Mean_Temperature</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="7" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="283.091">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
Higher temperatures result in a larger fractional damage to the economy.

Polarity Reasoning: 
The economic damage function maps global mean temperature increases to a fractional reduction in economic output.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>Higher temperatures result in a larger fractional damage to the economy.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>The economic damage function maps global mean temperature increases to a fractional reduction in economic output.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Global_Mean_Temperature</from>
					<to>Economic_Damages_Fraction</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="8" font_size="18pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="180">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
A higher fraction of economic damages directly reduces net economic output.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Net output is calculated by multiplying gross output by the undamaged fraction and subtracting abatement costs.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>A higher fraction of economic damages directly reduces net economic output.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Net output is calculated by multiplying gross output by the undamaged fraction and subtracting abatement costs.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Economic_Damages_Fraction</from>
					<to>Net_Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="9" font_size="18pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="323.794">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
Higher abatement costs directly subtract from net economic output.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Net economic output is reduced by the costs of carbon abatement.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>Higher abatement costs directly subtract from net economic output.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Net economic output is reduced by the costs of carbon abatement.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Abatement_Cost</from>
					<to>Net_Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="10" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="91.7471">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
An increase in net economic output leads to more capital investment, assuming a stable savings rate.

Polarity Reasoning: 
A portion of net economic output is saved and reinvested back into the physical capital stock.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>An increase in net economic output leads to more capital investment, assuming a stable savings rate.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>A portion of net economic output is saved and reinvested back into the physical capital stock.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Net_Economic_Output</from>
					<to>Capital_Investment</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="11" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="141.771">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
An increase in capital increases gross economic output.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Accumulated physical capital is an input to the production function that determines gross economic output.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>An increase in capital increases gross economic output.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Accumulated physical capital is an input to the production function that determines gross economic output.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Capital_Investment</from>
					<to>Gross_Economic_Output</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="12" font_size="18pt" polarity="+" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="31.6194">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
An increase in temperature leads to an increase in the rate at which permafrost melts.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Under the permafrost submodel, temperature increases speed up the thawing of frozen permafrost, which accelerates significantly past 2 degrees.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>An increase in temperature leads to an increase in the rate at which permafrost melts.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Under the permafrost submodel, temperature increases speed up the thawing of frozen permafrost, which accelerates significantly past 2 degrees.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Global_Mean_Temperature</from>
					<to>Permafrost_Thaw_Rate</to>
				</connector>
				<aux color="#919191" font_color="#919191" x="549.629" y="300" width="191.5" height="32" name="sequestration capacity">
					<shape type="name_only"/>
				</aux>
				<connector uid="16" font_size="18pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="142.359">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
Warming temperatures increase respiration rates in soil and vegetation, reducing net carbon uptake.

Polarity Reasoning: 
Higher temperatures decrease the net sequestration capacity of land and ocean carbon sinks due to respiration losses.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>Warming temperatures increase respiration rates in soil and vegetation, reducing net carbon uptake.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>Higher temperatures decrease the net sequestration capacity of land and ocean carbon sinks due to respiration losses.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>Global_Mean_Temperature</from>
					<to>sequestration_capacity</to>
				</connector>
				<connector uid="17" font_size="18pt" polarity="-" isee:polarity_placement="outside_mid_arc" angle="117.729">
					<isee:documentation>Reasoning:
A higher sequestration capacity removes more carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.

Polarity Reasoning: 
As sinks absorb more carbon, the overall atmospheric concentration is mitigated.</isee:documentation>
					<isee:reasoning>A higher sequestration capacity removes more carbon dioxide from the atmosphere.</isee:reasoning>
					<isee:polarity_reasoning>As sinks absorb more carbon, the overall atmospheric concentration is mitigated.</isee:polarity_reasoning>
					<from>sequestration_capacity</from>
					<to>Atmospheric_CO2_Concentration</to>
				</connector>
